WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.500
A plant's range ends at a headland.

2
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:07.840
Why does it stop there?

3
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.380
Is it the cold, the neighbours, or seeds never getting there?

4
00:00:13.383 --> 00:00:18.603
The first film drew a niche as the conditions where lambda stays at or above one

5
00:00:18.603 --> 00:00:19.603
without immigrants.

6
00:00:20.783 --> 00:00:22.223
Now we test an edge directly.

7
00:00:23.417 --> 00:00:25.817
Each answer is a different kind of limit.

8
00:00:27.597 --> 00:00:29.137
Conditions make an abiotic limit.

9
00:00:30.437 --> 00:00:32.337
Other species make a biotic limit.

10
00:00:33.577 --> 00:00:35.917
Never arriving makes a dispersal limit.

11
00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.730
The direct test is to plant the species beyond the edge and follow it for years.

12
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:45.090
But plant the same way inside the range too.

13
00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:50.530
Transplanting itself is a shock, and a bad year can kill plants anywhere.

14
00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.630
Then split every site in two.

15
00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.250
On randomly chosen plots, clear away the neighbouring plants.

16
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:00.790
On the others, leave them.

17
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.020
Count survival, growth and seed production across the whole life cycle.

18
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.880
From those, estimate lambda for every plot.

19
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.220
One season's survival is not enough.

20
00:01:14.283 --> 00:01:15.903
First possible result.

21
00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:21.163
Lambda beyond the edge stays at or above one, with or without neighbours.

22
00:01:22.563 --> 00:01:24.003
The species can live there.

23
00:01:25.303 --> 00:01:28.803
Seeds have not arrived, or have not recolonised empty patches.

24
00:01:29.983 --> 00:01:34.163
The dune plant looks like this case, though its neighbours were never cleared.

25
00:01:35.523 --> 00:01:40.163
Planted beyond its northern edge, the population more than tripled in twelve to

26
00:01:40.163 --> 00:01:41.163
fourteen years.

27
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:48.143
Second result. Beyond the edge, lambda falls below one only on plots where

28
00:01:48.143 --> 00:01:49.143
neighbours remain.

29
00:01:50.363 --> 00:01:51.463
Cleared plots do fine.

30
00:01:52.763 --> 00:01:54.483
Other species are setting the limit.

31
00:01:55.533 --> 00:01:59.533
Third result. The plots inside the range grow.

32
00:02:00.713 --> 00:02:03.953
Beyond the edge, plots shrink whether or not neighbours were cleared.

33
00:02:05.213 --> 00:02:06.413
What does that implicate?

34
00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:14.850
The conditions beyond the edge.

35
00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.610
Climate is a strong candidate.

36
00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:23.490
But herbivores, pathogens or missing soil partners need their own tests first.

37
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:30.980
One more result. Every plot fails, inside the range as well as beyond it.

38
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.020
Then the test cannot single out the edge.

39
00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.980
Handling, the seed or a bad year may be to blame.

40
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.880
Check them, then repeat the study.

41
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.810
So here is the reading rule.

42
00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:49.730
Compare lambda beyond the edge with lambda inside the range, on plots with and

43
00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:50.730
without neighbours.

44
00:02:51.533 --> 00:02:56.393
That comparison separates access, neighbours and conditions beyond the edge.

45
00:02:57.633 --> 00:03:00.633
A map of where the species grows cannot do it on its own.

46
00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:06.127
Plant beyond the edge, with the same planting inside, and measure lambda.

47
00:03:07.447 --> 00:03:11.567
Where it replaces itself beyond the edge, the range stops short of the niche.
